$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q7: What is the purpose of imagePullSecrets in a pod spec?</h5><ol><li>Encrypt pod logs</li><li>Provide registry credentials so kubelet can pull from private registries like ghcr.io/myorg</li><li>Sign container images</li><li>Scan images for vulnerabilities</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Private registry authentication.</strong> imagePullSecrets reference a kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson Secret containing registry credentials. Without this, kubelet cannot pull from private registries.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q8: What is the advantage of multi-stage Docker builds for security?</h5><ol><li>Faster builds</li><li>Builder stage with compilers/tools is discarded — final image contains ONLY runtime artifacts, dramatically reducing attack surface</li><li>Smaller image tags</li><li>Multi-stage builds are faster</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Builder tools discarded.</strong> Multi-stage builds separate build environment (gcc, pip, git) from runtime. Build tools (common attack vectors) never appear in the final image. Result: smaller, more secure images with fewer CVEs.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q9: What does immutable tags in a registry prevent?</h5><ol><li>Image deletion</li><li>Tag-swapping attacks — once v1.0.0 is pushed, it can never be overwritten with a different image</li><li>Image scanning</li><li>Image pulling</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Tag-swapping attacks.</strong> Without immutable tags, an attacker could push a malicious image tagged v1.0.0 over the legitimate one. Immutable tags prevent overwrites — each tag points to exactly one image forever.</p></details></div>')
