$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q4: What is the 5-step threat modeling process?</h5><ol><li>Scan -> Patch -> Monitor -> Report -> Repeat</li><li>Scope -> Decompose -> Identify (STRIDE) -> Score (DREAD) -> Mitigate</li><li>Build -> Test -> Deploy -> Monitor -> Scale</li><li>Plan -> Code -> Build -> Test -> Release</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Scope->Decompose->Identify->Score->Mitigate.</strong> 1.Define scope (assets), 2.Decompose (data flows, boundaries), 3.Identify threats (STRIDE per component), 4.Analyze/score (DREAD), 5.Define countermeasures.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q5: What does the T in STRIDE stand for and how is it mitigated in K8s?</h5><ol><li>Trust — mitigated by NetworkPolicies</li><li>Tampering — mitigated by image signing (Cosign), admission control, and readOnlyRootFilesystem</li><li>Threat — mitigated by Falco</li><li>Token — mitigated by RBAC</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Tampering.</strong> Tampering = unauthorized modification. K8s mitigations: Cosign for image integrity, admission webhooks preventing unauthorized changes, readOnlyRootFilesystem blocking container filesystem writes.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q6: MITRE ATT&CK: T1610 (Deploy Container) maps to which tactic?</h5><ol><li>Execution</li><li>Initial Access — attacker deploys a malicious container to gain foothold</li><li>Persistence</li><li>Discovery</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Initial Access.</strong> T1610 (Deploy Container) is an Initial Access technique where an attacker deploys their own malicious container. Mitigate with admission control (Kyverno/OPA) and registry restrictions.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q7: What does the R in STRIDE represent and how is it mitigated?</h5><ol><li>Risk — mitigated by DREAD</li><li>Repudiation — mitigated by audit logging that proves who did what</li><li>Recovery — mitigated by backups</li><li>Reconnaissance — mitigated by NetworkPolicies</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Repudiation.</strong> Repudiation = denying an action was performed. Mitigated by comprehensive audit logging (who, what, when, where) with immutable storage. Audit logs provide non-repudiation.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q8: What DREAD score would trigger immediate remediation?</h5><ol><li>Any score above 0</li><li>Total score above 30/50 — indicates high damage, reproducibility, exploitability, many users affected, and easy discoverability</li><li>Only score of 50</li><li>Score doesn''t matter for prioritization</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Score above 30 = immediate fix.</strong> DREAD ≥ 30 = high priority. 40+ = critical (all users, severe damage, easy to exploit). 20-30 = fix soon. Under 20 = monitor. Use scoring to prioritize limited remediation resources.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q9: How does MITRE ATT&CK Container matrix help with K8s defense?</h5><ol><li>It lists software bugs</li><li>Maps real attacker techniques to specific K8s mitigations — each technique has documented detection and prevention methods</li><li>It is a compliance checklist</li><li>It is a scoring system</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Attack-to-defense mapping.</strong> MITRE ATT&CK for Containers maps 9 tactics and 40+ techniques to specific K8s controls. For each technique, it documents: how attackers do it, how to detect it (Falco rules), and how to prevent it (PSS, RBAC, NetworkPolicy).</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q10: What is the difference between an attack vector and attack surface?</h5><ol><li>Same thing — interchangeable</li><li>Attack vector = PATH an attacker takes to compromise (e.g., exposed API). Attack surface = ALL possible entry points combined</li><li>Attack surface is the path; vector is all points</li><li>Neither concept applies to K8s</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Path vs total area.</strong> Attack vector = specific path (e.g., compromised CI pipeline). Attack surface = sum of all vectors (API server, kubelet, etcd, pods, registry, supply chain). Minimize attack surface by removing unnecessary components.</p></details></div>')
Add-ChapterQs 24 $q

# Ch25: +7 - Supply Chain Compliance
$q=@()
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q4: What does Executive Order 14028 require for US government software?</h5><ol><li>Open source only</li><li>SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) and secure development attestation</li><li>Written in Rust</li><li>No third-party dependencies</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. SBOM + attestation.</strong> EO 14028 mandates SBOMs for all software sold to the US federal government, plus attestation of secure development practices. This drove the industry-wide adoption of SBOM standards.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q5: What does the EU Cyber Resilience Act require?</h5><ol><li>Only encryption</li><li>Security by design, SBOMs, vulnerability disclosure, and 5-year security support for all software products</li><li>Only documentation</li><li>Only incident reporting</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Full lifecycle security.</strong> EU CRA mandates: security by design/default, SBOM generation, vulnerability disclosure process, and minimum 5-year security support. Phased rollout through 2027. Applies to ALL software sold in EU.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q6: What is drift detection in GitOps compliance?</h5><ol><li>Detecting network latency drift</li><li>Detecting when live cluster configuration differs from the Git source of truth</li><li>Detecting memory leaks</li><li>Detecting pod restarts</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Configuration drift from Git.</strong> In GitOps, Git is the single source of truth. Drift detection (ArgoCD, Flux, Kubescape) alerts when someone manually changes something on the cluster — critical for audit compliance.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q7: What evidence does a Rekor transparency log provide?</h5><ol><li>Vulnerability scan results</li><li>Immutable, timestamped proof of who signed what artifact and when — cannot be altered retroactively</li><li>Compliance report</li><li>SBOM inventory</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Immutable signing proof.</strong> Rekor stores cryptographically verifiable records of every artifact signing. The log is append-only — once recorded, entries cannot be modified. Provides auditor-ready proof of supply chain integrity.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q8: What should a compliance evidence package contain for auditors?</h5><ol><li>Only a checklist</li><li>Kubescape PDF report + kube-bench CIS score + SBOM archive + Rekor signing log + Git change history</li><li>Only the audit log</li><li>Only a self-assessment</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Multi-source evidence.</strong> Complete package: framework compliance report (Kubescape), CIS benchmark score (kube-bench), dependency inventory (SBOM archive), signing proof (Rekor log), change history (Git). Automated, timestamped, immutable.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q9: How does continuous compliance differ from point-in-time audits?</h5><ol><li>Same thing</li><li>Continuous compliance runs automated checks constantly — not just before an audit. Drift is detected immediately</li><li>Point-in-time is more reliable</li><li>Continuous is less secure</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Always-on verification.</strong> Point-in-time audits check compliance once (e.g., annually). Continuous compliance runs automated scans daily/hourly — detecting non-compliance immediately, not months later when auditors arrive.</p></details></div>')
$q+=,@('                    <div class="drill-scenario"><h5>Q10: What is the complete supply chain security automation pipeline?</h5><ol><li>Only image scanning</li><li>Commit -> SBOM (Syft) -> Vuln scan (Trivy) -> Sign (Cosign) -> Provenance (SLSA) -> Admission verify (Kyverno)</li><li>Only image signing</li><li>Only admission control</li></ol></div>','                    <div class="drill-solution"><details><summary>✅ Answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: 2. Full automated pipeline.</strong> Every commit triggers: SBOM generation (Syft), vulnerability scanning (Trivy/Grype), image signing (Cosign with OIDC), SLSA provenance generation, and admission control verification (Kyverno). Fully automated, no manual steps.</p></details></div>')
Add-ChapterQs 25 $q

